1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,510
I had been hanging around for a few years with a very party-time crowd, and my life was on a downward slide.
我跟一帮成天派对狂欢的人已经混了几年，生活一路走下坡路。

2
00:00:08,310 --> 00:00:11,550
I had gotten a Ph.D. and a divorce simultaneously.
我拿到博士学位的同时，也离了婚。

3
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:15,210
The fancy academic job never materialized.
那份光鲜的学术工作始终没有着落。

4
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:23,440
Soon I was playing flute in a street band, eating donated sandwiches, and spending every night in clubs dancing the night away.
很快，我就在一支街头乐队里吹长笛，吃别人捐的三明治，每晚都泡在夜店里，一跳就是一整夜。

5
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:29,100
In a few years I was living in the mountains in a shack, and none of my friends worked.
几年后，我住在山里的一间棚屋里，朋友中没有一个人上班。

6
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:37,160
When I saw the ad in the Sunday paper—BRAKEMEN WANTED—I thought of it as a chance to clean up my act and to get away.
我在周日的报纸上看到一则广告——“招聘制动员”，便把它看作一个改过自新、抽身离开的机会。

7
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:44,080
I felt that by doing work this dangerous, I would have to make a decision to live, to protect myself.
我觉得，干这么危险的工作，我就不得不下决心活下去，保护好自己。

8
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:50,950
I would have to choose to stay alive every day, to hang on to the sides of those freight cars for dear life.
我每天都得选择保住性命，拼命抓牢那些货车车厢的侧面。

9
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:54,430
The railroad transformed the metaphor of my life.
铁路彻底改变了我人生的隐喻。

10
00:00:55,230 --> 00:00:59,570
Nine thousand tons moving at sixty miles an hour into the fearful night.
九千吨，以每小时六十英里的速度，驶进令人生畏的黑夜。

11
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:03,920
I now would ride that image, trying to stay alive within it.
从此，我要乘上这个意象，努力在其中活下去。

12
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:16,260
I know that later, when I sat behind the moving train in the darkness of the caboose, window open and the unknown fragrances of the land filling the space, the blackness of the night was my friend.
我知道，后来我坐在行驶的列车尾部，守车里一片黑暗，车窗敞开，大地上不知名的芬芳弥漫其间，那时，漆黑的夜就是我的朋友。

13
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:21,400
It felt good to be powerless and carried along by the destiny of that motion.
无能为力，任由那运动的宿命带着自己向前，感觉真好。

14
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:23,730
I felt happy and at peace.
我感到快乐而安宁。

15
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:25,890
I was where I belonged.
我到了自己该在的地方。

16
00:01:26,690 --> 00:01:29,640
The railroad didn’t believe in lengthy formal training.
铁路公司并不推崇冗长的正规培训。

17
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:43,200
They offered a two-week class which covered the book of rules, a three-hundred-page document with a dual purpose—to keep trains from running into one another and to prevent any situation in which the company might get sued.
他们开设了一门为期两周的课程，讲授规章手册——一份三百页的文件，有双重目的：一是防止列车相互碰撞，二是防止出现任何可能让公司遭到起诉的情况。

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,270
Rules of the road which you had to learn were mixed in with rules which you had to ignore in order to get the work done.
你必须掌握的行车规则，跟那些为了把活儿干完而必须置之不理的规则混杂在一起。

19
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:59,810
But you had to know that you were ignoring a rule so that in the winter, when company officials had time to sneak around testing, you could work by the book.
但你必须清楚自己是在无视某条规则，这样一来，到了冬天公司主管有空偷偷摸摸四处抽查的时候，你就能照章办事。

20
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:03,880
The rulebook was also in a continuous state of revision.
规章手册还一直处于不断修订之中。

21
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:08,710
Revisions appeared in the timetable which you carried with you at all times.
修订内容会登在时刻表上，这份时刻表你随时都带在身上。

22
00:02:09,510 --> 00:02:14,620
Further revisions appeared in regular timetable bulletins which were posted at work.
更多的修订则登在定期发布的时刻表公告上，这些公告张贴在单位里。

23
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:25,420
Soon your rulebook resembled a scrapbook, with paragraphs crossed out, pages pasted in, and notes on changes which were then crossed out and changed weeks later.
很快，你的规章手册就像一本剪贴簿：有的段落划掉了，有的页面是贴进去的，还写着关于改动的注释，而几周后这些注释又被划掉、重新改过。

24
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:27,610
It drove you crazy.
这能把人逼疯。

25
00:02:28,410 --> 00:02:33,780
You always had to be on the lookout for a company official hiding in the bushes while you did your work.
干活的时候，你总得提防躲在灌木丛里的公司主管。

26
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:39,360
This individual would pop out and ask you questions about the latest rule revisions.
这个人会突然冒出来，问你一些有关最新规章修订的问题。

27
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:43,610
A notation of failure would then appear in your personal file.
随后，你的个人档案里就会出现一条不合格记录。

28
00:02:44,410 --> 00:02:51,150
These notations were referred to as “Brownies,” named after the official who devised the railroad demerit system.
这些记录被称为“布朗尼”，得名于设计出铁路记过制度的那位主管。

29
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:56,980
As trainmen were fond of pointing out, however, there was no merit system to go with it.
然而，正如列车员们喜欢指出的那样，并没有一套记功制度与之配套。

30
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:00,900
Out of seventeen student brakemen three of us were women.
十七名实习制动员中，我们有三人是女性。

31
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,710
This was a large percentage, comparatively.
相对而言，这是个很高的比例。

32
00:03:05,510 --> 00:03:10,390
The first women had been hired two years before, and they were around to give us advice.
第一批女性是两年前招进来的，她们都还在，能给我们出主意。

33
00:03:11,190 --> 00:03:16,420
The point was to get through the class, and get over the probationary period known as the “derail.”
要紧的是通过这门课，再闯过那段被称为“脱轨器”的试用期。

34
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:20,060
Then you were in the union and a railroader for life.
然后你就是工会会员，一辈子都是铁路人了。

35
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:28,830
Getting over the derail took sixty days, and if either the crews you worked with or the company officers had a complaint, you were out.
要闯过“脱轨器”这一关需要六十天；无论是和你共事的班组，还是公司主管，只要有意见，你就出局了。

36
00:03:29,630 --> 00:03:39,460
At the end of two weeks of classroom instruction, you bought a railroad watch, they gave you switch keys and a two-dollar lantern, and you marked up as extra board brakemen.
两周的课堂教学结束后，你买上一块铁路表，他们会发给你道岔钥匙和一盏两美元的提灯，然后你就登记上岗，成为候补制动员。

37
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:45,270
On the last day of class, they took us down to the freightyard to grapple with the equipment.
课程的最后一天，他们带我们去货运场，跟那些设备较劲。

38
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:57,690
We practiced getting on and off moving cars, climbing the ladders and cranking down the handbrakes, changing the eighty-five-pound knuckles that joined the cars together, and hand and lantern signals.
我们练习上下行驶中的车厢，爬梯子，摇紧手闸，更换把车厢连接起来的八十五磅重的车钩钩舌，还练习手势信号和提灯信号。

39
00:03:58,490 --> 00:04:03,660
These signals were the way members of the crew talked to each other, and they were an art form.
这些信号是班组成员彼此交谈的方式，也是一种艺术形式。

40
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:08,050
An old head could practically order a pizza from a half mile away.
老手隔着半英里简直都能点份比萨。

41
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:11,710
You would see lights, arcs and circles, stabs of light.
你会看到灯光、弧线、圆圈，还有一道道骤然闪出的光。

42
00:04:12,510 --> 00:04:13,530
It would repeat.
信号会重复一遍。

43
00:04:14,330 --> 00:04:15,950
You would stand there confused.
你会站在那里，一头雾水。

44
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:21,220
Finally you would walk down the track and find the foreman in a deep state of disgust.
最后，你会沿着铁轨走过去，发现领班厌恶至极。

45
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:30,140
“I told you to hang three cars, let two go to the runaround, one to the main, go through the crossovers, and line behind.
“我叫你挂三节车，放两节去绕行线，一节去正线，走渡线，再把身后的道岔扳回去。

46
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:33,360
Now can’t you read a signal, dummy?”
我说，你就不会看信号吗，笨蛋？”

47
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:39,280
The day after our practice session, I got into my car and tried to roll the window down.
练习课后的第二天，我坐进自己的车里，试着把车窗摇下来。

48
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:41,490
My arms didn’t work.
我的胳膊不听使唤了。

49
00:04:42,290 --> 00:04:46,100
This was my first moment of doubt about being able to do the job.
这是我第一次怀疑自己能否胜任这份工作。

50
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:53,240
It was hard to get the upper-body strength required to hang on and ride for long distances on the side of cars.
要紧紧扒住车厢侧面、随车行进很长的距离，就需要上身有力量，而这种力量很难练出来。

51
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:58,230
Terror at falling beneath the wheels was a big motivator, however.
不过，对跌落到车轮底下的恐惧，是一股强大的动力。

52
00:04:59,030 --> 00:05:00,330
Terror and ridicule.
恐惧，还有嘲笑。

53
00:05:01,130 --> 00:05:05,140
There was a lot of both during the probationary period and the student trips.
在试用期和实习出车期间，这两样都多得是。

54
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:10,450
On student trips we tagged along with a regular crew and tried to learn something.
实习出车时，我们跟着一个正式班组，努力学点东西。

55
00:05:11,250 --> 00:05:14,420
To me, what we were doing made no sense whatsoever.
在我看来，我们干的这些事完全莫名其妙。

56
00:05:15,220 --> 00:05:22,720
Just getting used to the equipment had me so disorientated that I had no idea where we had gone or how the crew did anything.
光是适应那些设备，就把我弄得晕头转向，既不知道我们去过哪里，也不知道班组的活儿都是怎么干的。

57
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:30,040
One of the crew suggested to me that I go to a toy store and look at the model trains, to see how switches work.
班组里有个人建议我去玩具店看看模型火车，好弄明白道岔是怎么工作的。

58
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:39,040
They say, though, that whatever you start out doing railroading, it gets imprinted, and that’s what you are most comfortable doing from then on.
不过，人们说，干铁路这一行，无论你起步时做的是什么，都会在你身上深深打下烙印，从那以后，你做那件事最得心应手。

59
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:43,580
I couldn’t have picked a better place to break in than Watsonville Junction.
要说入行的地方，我再也挑不出比沃森维尔枢纽站更好的了。

60
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:47,630
It was old-time, local-freight, full-crew switching.
那里的活儿是老派的调车作业：地方货运，满员班组。

61
00:05:48,430 --> 00:05:50,330
Kicking cars and passing signs.
溜放车厢，传递信号。

62
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:56,320
The basic stuff that you have to learn at first or you never get no matter how long you’re out here.
这是基本功，你得一开始就学会，不然不管在这儿干多久，你都永远弄不懂。
